      "type": "tool",
      "heading": "Tool: current-law mechanics",
      "result": "26 U.S.C. §24 text available in the draft context states subsection (d)(1)(B)(i) is applied by substituting $2,500 for $3,000 under current law. IRS public guidance for 2023 states the CTC initial amount is $2,000 per qualifying child and the maximum ACTC refundable amount for each qualifying child increased to $1,600."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "heading": "Base rate",
      "text": "Base-rate evidence is incomplete: the draft run identified the official IRS Table 3.3 XLS files but could not parse the numeric ACTC return-count history. I therefore do not claim a numeric official 6-24 print history. The usable prior is a policy-mechanics prior anchored to a post-TCJA ACTC claimant pool with a material upward adjustment for lowering the earned-income threshold from $2,500 to $1."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "heading": "Math derivation",
      "text": "Limited-evidence computation: start from a low-20s-million claimant-pool prior for the existing ACTC/refundable CTC line; add roughly 2 million returns for families with positive earned income below or near the current $2,500 threshold and families whose refundable amount becomes positive after the threshold cut; round to 23.0 million. Because no parsed official history was available, use a deliberately wide 80% interval of -5.0/+8.0 million around the point, giving 18.0 to 31.0 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "heading": "Prior/update/interval",
      "text": "Prior/update/interval: prior = limited-evidence policy-mechanics claimant-pool prior for the IRS Table 3.3 refundable/additional child tax credit count; historical sample = official IRS Table 3.3 files identified but numeric values not parsed in the draft; adjustment components = lower earned-income threshold to no more than $1 increases eligibility/positive refundable amounts for very-low-earnings families, partly offset by filing and qualifying-child constraints; interval method = wide fallback interval due missing parsed history; implied bounds = 18.0 to 31.0 million."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "heading": "Counter-consideration",
      "text": "Upside outside the interval would require the threshold change to be paired with broader refundability, higher per-child caps, major filing-outreach gains, or recession-driven movement into refundable-credit eligibility. Downside outside the interval would require weak take-up among very-low-income families, restrictive implementation rules, narrower qualifying-child rules, or the conditional legislation passing in a form that lowers the threshold but constrains eligibility elsewhere."
